WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.857
Industrial systems use several recurring communication patterns.

00:00:03.857 --> 00:00:09.530
Recognizing them helps defenders distinguish routine control behavior from meaningful change.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:19.741
The patterns are not tied to one protocol, and a single site may use several of them at the same time.

00:00:19.741 --> 00:00:26.775
Polling is common when one system repeatedly asks devices for current values or status.

00:00:26.775 --> 00:00:32.447
The responding device returns requested data, creating a stable request-and-response rhythm.

00:00:32.447 --> 00:00:42.204
Command-and-response communication is similar, but the request may direct an operation and the response confirms acceptance, completion, or an error.

00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:46.061
Publish-and-subscribe designs separate data producers from consumers.

00:00:46.061 --> 00:00:53.549
A publisher makes selected information available, and subscribers receive updates without individually polling the source.

00:00:53.549 --> 00:01:03.306
This can support efficient distribution, but defenders still need to understand which publishers, subscribers, topics, and data flows are approved.

00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:08.979
Controller-to-controller communication coordinates related equipment, interlocks, sequencing, or shared process state.

00:01:08.979 --> 00:01:17.828
Supervisory communication connects HMIs or SCADA servers with controllers so operators can view conditions and issue authorized actions.

00:01:17.828 --> 00:01:22.593
These pathways may be highly repetitive and operationally critical.

00:01:22.593 --> 00:01:28.265
Historians collect measurements, alarms, and events for trending, reporting, and analysis.

00:01:28.265 --> 00:01:35.753
Their collection paths often cross architectural levels, so interfaces and replication routes deserve careful design.

00:01:35.753 --> 00:01:40.064
Engineering access is less frequent but more privileged.

00:01:40.064 --> 00:01:45.737
It may support configuration, diagnostics, firmware maintenance, or controller project changes.

00:01:45.737 --> 00:01:53.225
A useful baseline describes expected roles, peers, direction, frequency, and operating windows for each pattern.

00:01:53.225 --> 00:02:02.982
New controller relationships, unexpected publishers, a historian reaching into an unusual zone, or engineering access outside maintenance may warrant review.

00:02:02.982 --> 00:02:09.108
The pattern identifies what changed; operators, engineers, and change records explain why.
